 Measuring whether synthetic records help a classifier requires real records that the generator has never seen. Each dataset is therefore divided into a training set, given to the generator, and a test set of 20\% of the records, used only to score classifiers. Because a test set of 39 to 154 records makes a single division unreliable, the division is repeated ten times with different random seeds, and means and standard deviations are reported. In each repetition, the number of synthetic records $N$ equals the number of training records, so augmentation doubles the training set. Ten classifiers covering the main model families are trained: logistic regression (LR), support vector machine (SVM), $k$-nearest neighbours ($k$NN), Gaussian naive Bayes (NB), decision tree (DT), random forest (RF), gradient boosting (GB), AdaBoost, XGBoost and a multilayer perceptron (MLP). Using several families avoids conclusions tied to one learner, since interpolated records can help a nearest-neighbour classifier and harm a boosted ensemble. Each classifier is trained on real records only, on synthetic records only, and on real plus synthetic records, and all three are scored on the same test set. XGBoost failed on liver disease in every repetition because the labels are coded $\{1,2\}$, so 59 of the 60 classifier--dataset combinations are reported.
 
