 The control layer and the reliability of the generated datasets are directly related: without it, the same LLM never delivered the configured number of records; with it, every dataset was complete, at 1.9\% more tokens and with unchanged quality. Table~\ref{tab:raw} compares direct prompting with the reference \system on the same prompts, context and divisions. Direct prompting delivered on average 87.6\% of the configured records, fell short in 50 of 60 runs and overshot in the other 10, and lost an entire batch to each of ten malformed responses. \system delivers exactly $N$ records because its completion loop requests the missing ones and truncates any excess, and its parser recovers malformed responses instead of discarding them. Fidelity, privacy and utility were the same for both configurations (JSD 72.7; attack AUC 0.517 versus 0.526; F1 change $-0.04$ versus $-0.14$ pp, within the noise floor below), so the difference lies not in the records but in whether the dataset that was configured is delivered.
 
+A natural objection is that the structured outputs now offered by LLM providers, which constrain decoding to a JSON schema, make such a layer unnecessary. They do not replace it. With the same prompts and a strict schema fixing the column names, the numerical types and the observed categories, every response parsed and no value had the wrong type, but the LLM returned on average only 77.7\% of the configured records (51--97\% over divisions), never the full dataset; 11 requests failed outright, and the run used 21\% more tokens than \system. The schema also weakened the dependence between features: correlation fidelity fell from 72.6 to 63.0 and the C2ST accuracy rose from 0.878 to 0.943, so the records became easier to tell from real ones, while the F1 change ($+0.35$ pp) stayed within the noise floor. Constrained decoding therefore guarantees the format of each record, but not the size of the dataset, which is what the completion loop of the control layer provides.
